<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<layout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      PLAYER page - one stream of the pager - migrated to the frozen 3.6.6
      canonical design (Phase B).

      Source: canonical `PLAYER` / `PLAYER_dark`, `Player Section`. The page starts
      where the frozen frame puts the album art region, at 79; the header and the
      swipe dots are chrome and live in fragment_player.xml. Offsets inside the
      page are therefore the frozen ones minus 79:

        16   album art   239x239 r20      292   track info      363   controls

      The frozen screen has no full-bleed stream artwork behind any of this - the
      background is a flat `background` fill and the only backdrop is the one
      behind the album art. The per-stream background images and the decorative
      artist frame have no canonical counterpart and are gone from this screen.

      It scrolls for the same reason HOME does: the frozen frame is 1022 tall,
      taller than any phone, so a fixed design has to be presented on a viewport
      that varies. Phase C's Broadcast History card lands in the space below.
    -->
    <androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView
        android:id="@+id/stream_scroll"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:clipToPadding="false"
        android:fillViewport="true"
        android:scrollbars="none"
        tools:context=".fragments.MyataStreamFragment">

        <!--
          Each block is anchored by its own absolute offset from the page top -
          artwork 16, artist 324, controls 363 - rather than stacked, so a box that
          needs more height than the frozen one cannot push the blocks below it out
          of place.

          The track title is the one exception, and it proves the rule: it is
          anchored by its bottom to the artist instead, so its second line grows
          up into the gap under the artwork. Everything else stays exactly where
          the frozen frame puts it. The measurements are on main_song below.
        -->
        <androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
            android:id="@+id/stream_content_container"
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:paddingBottom="@dimen/player_margin">

            <!--
              `Current Track Artwork`, 239x239 r20 with a 1px outline and an image
              fill set to FILL. The frozen `Background+Blur` behind it is a 229
              square under an opaque 239 one, so it never shows; its colour is the
              card's background instead, which is what fills the card while the
              artwork loads.
            -->
            <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
                android:id="@+id/artwork_card"
                style="@style/PlayerArtworkCard"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_artwork_margin_top"
                app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent">

                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/photo"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="match_parent"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/player_artwork"
                    android:scaleType="centerCrop"
                    android:src="@drawable/zaglushka_logo" />
            </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

            <!--
              `Track Info`: the title in Heading 1 above the artist, gap 8. The app
              had these the other way round - the artist in the 24 Black slot and
              the title in the 18 Regular one - which is the reverse of the frozen
              frame and of the Mini Player. The ids keep their meaning: main_song is
              the track title, main_author the artist, and the clipboard copy still
              reads "artist - title" from them.

              The artist is one line, ellipsized: the frozen box is 18 tall with
              clipping on, and ellipsis is that decision without the clipping.

              The **title takes up to two**, and it grows upward. It is the primary
              content on this screen, and at one line it rendered "WHERE CAN I PUT
              ALL MY LOVE" as "WHERE CAN I PUT ALL MY ..." at 390dp - a title that
              fits perfectly well in two.

              Upward is the only direction available, and the numbers say so.
              Measured at 390dp: a line is 29.3 tall and a second costs 23.8, the
              artist starts at 324 and the controls at 363, so the whole space below
              the title is 2.9 before the artist and 17.0 before the controls. A
              second line growing downward would cross the artist by 20.9. Above the
              title there is 36.8 of deliberate gap under the artwork, and 23.8 fits
              inside it with 13.0 to spare - so the title's **bottom** is anchored
              and the box extends up into that gap.

              What this buys, beyond the reported title: artist and controls never
              move. They keep the frozen 324 and 363 whatever the title does, which
              is what the top-anchored version could not have offered. It is also
              the better failure direction for a large system font scale, which now
              grows into 36.8 of gap instead of into 2.9.

              Two, not three: a third line needs another 23.8 and would start at
              244.2, which is above the artwork's own bottom edge at 255. maxLines
              is what keeps that from being reachable, and a title too long for two
              lines still ellipsizes on the second.

              The frozen one-line position is unchanged: bottom at (324 - 3) with a
              29.3 box puts the top at 291.7, which is the frozen 292.
              PlayerLayoutTest pins it.
            -->
            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/main_song"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:layout_marginBottom="@dimen/player_track_title_artist_gap"
                android:ellipsize="end"
                android:gravity="center"
                android:maxLines="2"
                android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.Myata.Montserrat.Black.24_24"
                android:textColor="@color/text_primary"
                app:layout_constraintBottom_toTopOf="@+id/main_author"
                app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                tools:text="WHAT YOU KNOW" />

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/main_author"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_track_artist_top"
                android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:ellipsize="end"
                android:gravity="center"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.Myata.Montserrat.Regular.18_18"
                android:textColor="@color/text_secondary"
                app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
                tools:text="TWO DOOR CINEMA CLUB" />

            <!--
              `Controls`, 358x80 with 32 of side padding: the 80x80 play/pause
              centred, and a 49x54 slot at each edge.

              Phase B puts the existing favourite in the frozen `like` slot and the
              existing History entry in the `dislike` one. No dislike control is
              added, and History keeps working exactly as it does today until Phase
              C brings the Broadcast History card onto this screen.
            -->
            <androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
                android:id="@+id/player_controls"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="@dimen/player_controls_height"
                android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_controls_top"
                android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/player_margin"
                app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent">

                <!--
                  The frozen `like`: a filled thumbs-up, 24.5x23.33, 12 from the
                  top of its 49x54 slot. It replaces the heart the app drew here,
                  which had no counterpart in the design at all. The frozen frame
                  records one visual and no states, so the collection toggle is
                  carried by the tint - `text_primary` off, `primary` on - and the
                  geometry never changes.
                -->
                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/btn_favorite"
                    style="@style/PlayerLikeSlot"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/player_controls_inset"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/player_favorite_add"
                    android:src="@drawable/ic_player_like"
                    app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
                    app:tint="@color/player_like" />

                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/btn_play"
                    android:layout_width="@dimen/player_play_size"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/player_play_size"
                    android:background="@drawable/bg_player_play_pause"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/player_play"
                    android:scaleType="center"
                    android:src="@drawable/ic_player_play"
                    app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
                    app:tint="@color/player_play_glyph" />

                <!--
                  The frozen `dislike`, now installed. The slot held the History
                  entry from #42 onwards - "no new feedback behaviour is
                  invented" - because there was nothing behind a Dislike to
                  record. There is now: the reaction model stores NEUTRAL, LIKED
                  and DISLIKED, and this is the control for the third one.

                  It takes `PlayerLikeSlot`, the same style as `like`: the frozen
                  frame reads both side slots identically (`like`/`dislike >
                  Container > Icon`, 24.5x23.33, 12 from the top of a 49x54
                  slot), so the two are one geometry, and the trailing slot no
                  longer needs a style of its own. The colour is the frozen row's
                  own `player_control_action` at rest and `primary` when the
                  track is disliked - the same active tint `like` uses, because
                  the frozen frame records no state for either and inventing a
                  second one would be design this screen has not been given.

                  The History section stays exactly where Phase C put it, inline
                  further down this page; what goes is the shortcut that scrolled
                  to it, which was only ever this slot's stand-in.
                -->
                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/btn_dislike"
                    style="@style/PlayerLikeSlot"
                    android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/player_controls_inset"
                    android:contentDescription="@string/player_dislike_add"
                    android:src="@drawable/ic_player_dislike"
                    app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                    app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent"
                    app:tint="@color/player_control_action" />

                <!--
                  Connecting and re-buffering. It replaces the glyph, not the
                  control: `btn_play` keeps drawing its 80x80 primary surface
                  underneath, this is constrained to that surface's four edges so
                  it is centred inside it, and it takes the frozen glyph box so
                  nothing moves or changes size between Play, connecting and
                  Pause. Declared after `btn_play`, so it draws on top of it.

                  It takes the glyph's own colour, `player_play_glyph`, because it
                  stands in for the glyph: #F8F9FA on a #1C4771 surface in Light
                  and #0F253E on a #5FD9B4 one in Dark. Legible only because the
                  fill is behind it - see PlayerControl for what hiding the
                  surface used to do to it.

                  Material's CircularProgressIndicator rather than a plain
                  ProgressBar, for one reason: stroke weight. The platform's
                  indeterminate drawable is a VectorDrawable whose strokeWidth is
                  fixed against a fixed viewport, so its stroke is a constant
                  fraction of its diameter and cannot be set - it painted 2.17dp
                  against the Material glyphs' 3.97dp wall, and read visibly
                  thinner than the control it stands in for. `trackThickness` is
                  the only way to state that number, and this is the Material 3
                  component for it, which is what the glyphs are now too.

                  Diameter and box are deliberately unchanged: the drawn circle
                  keeps the 22.78dp the platform drawable painted and the view
                  keeps its 27.33dp box - see dimens.xml for how the two are held
                  together. The track is transparent so this stays an arc on the
                  surface rather than a full ring, which is what it was.
                -->
                <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.CircularProgressIndicator
                    android:id="@+id/loading_spinner"
                    android:layout_width="@dimen/player_play_icon"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/player_play_icon"
                    android:indeterminate="true"
                    app:indicatorColor="@color/player_play_glyph"
                    app:indicatorInset="@dimen/player_play_spinner_inset"
                    app:indicatorSize="@dimen/player_play_spinner_diameter"
                    app:trackColor="@android:color/transparent"
                    app:trackThickness="@dimen/player_play_spinner_thickness"
                    android:visibility="gone"
                    app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="@id/btn_play"
                    app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="@id/btn_play"
                    app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="@id/btn_play"
                    app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="@id/btn_play" />
            </androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>

            <!--
              `Broadcast History Section` (Phase C) - light node 2396:30784, dark
              2444:18288. 358 wide at 552 of the frozen frame, which is 473 on the
              page, 30 below where `Controls` ends.

              This is the one block on the page that is NOT anchored absolutely.
              Everything above it is positioned by its own offset from the page
              top so that a text box needing more height grows into the gap above
              the next block instead of pushing it down - see dimens.xml. That
              trick needs a next block to grow into, and there is nothing below
              this one: it is the end of the page, its height is HUG in the frozen
              frame too, and its content is real history rather than three mock
              rows. So it starts at the frozen 473 and is allowed to run as long as
              the history it is holding.

              The frozen section is 374 tall with three rows in it. Ten rows is
              about 1000, and thirty about 2600, which the NestedScrollView above
              was already there to carry.
            -->
            <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
                android:id="@+id/history_section"
                style="@style/PlayerHistoryCard"
                android:layout_width="0dp"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/player_margin"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_history_top"
                android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/player_margin"
                app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
                app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent">

                <LinearLayout
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:orientation="vertical"
                    android:padding="@dimen/player_history_padding">

                    <!--
                      `Margin > Container > Heading 2`, 24/32 Bold on
                      `text_primary`. Montserrat: section headings are Montserrat
                      by the typography contract, at the weight the frozen node
                      states. The 32 box is a minimum for the same reason the
                      track info's boxes are - Figma honours a declared line
                      height literally and Android cannot clip to it.

                      Same string as the control in the `dislike` slot: one name
                      for one thing.
                    -->
                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/history_heading"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:gravity="center_vertical"
                        android:minHeight="@dimen/player_history_heading_height"
                        android:text="@string/player_history"
                        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.Myata.Montserrat.Bold.24_32"
                        android:textColor="@color/text_primary" />

                    <!--
                      The frozen `Ещё` button in the heading row is
                      `visible: false` on both pages and is not drawn here.
                    -->

                    <!--
                      `List`. nestedScrollingEnabled=false because the page above
                      already scrolls and two nested scrollers would fight; with
                      the list at wrap_content inside it, this measures every row
                      it holds, which is what the frozen HUG section does and what
                      the 30-entry ceiling keeps affordable.
                    -->
                    <androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
                        android:id="@+id/history_list"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_history_heading_gap"
                        android:nestedScrollingEnabled="false"
                        android:overScrollMode="never"
                        tools:itemCount="3"
                        tools:listitem="@layout/item_player_history_track" />

                    <!--
                      Empty, and the failed-request state with it: the repository
                      returns an empty list rather than raising, so the two are one
                      state. It takes the list's own top gap so the section keeps
                      its shape when it holds no rows.
                    -->
                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/history_empty"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_history_heading_gap"
                        android:gravity="center"
                        android:minHeight="@dimen/player_history_row_height"
                        android:text="@string/player_history_empty"
                        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.Myata.Onest.Regular.17_28"
                        android:textColor="@color/text_secondary"
                        android:visibility="gone"
                        tools:visibility="gone" />

                    <!--
                      Loading. The frozen frame has nothing to say about it, so it
                      takes the same band the first row would and the section does
                      not resize when the rows arrive. Only ever shown when there
                      is nothing up yet - a refresh over existing rows leaves them
                      alone rather than making the page jump.
                    -->
                    <ProgressBar
                        android:id="@+id/history_loading"
                        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_height="@dimen/player_history_row_height"
                        android:layout_gravity="center_horizontal"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_history_heading_gap"
                        android:indeterminateTint="@color/primary"
                        android:visibility="gone"
                        tools:visibility="gone" />

                    <!--
                      `Button:margin > Button`, 324x54 r12, 16 above it. Montserrat
                      Medium 22/28: the typography contract puts buttons and CTAs
                      in Montserrat Medium at 22, which is what the frozen node's
                      22 with its pre-migration Muller weight maps to.

                      It is hidden, not disabled, when there is nothing left to
                      show - the frozen frame draws it over a list that has more
                      behind it, and a permanently inert control is what the brief
                      rules out.
                    -->
                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/history_show_more"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/player_history_more_gap"
                        android:background="@drawable/bg_player_history_more"
                        android:clickable="true"
                        android:focusable="true"
                        android:foreground="?attr/selectableItemBackground"
                        android:gravity="center"
                        android:minHeight="@dimen/player_history_more_height"
                        android:text="@string/player_history_show_more"
                        android:textAppearance="@style/TextAppearance.Myata.Montserrat.Medium.22_28"
                        android:textColor="@color/player_history_more_label"
                        android:visibility="gone"
                        tools:visibility="visible" />
                </LinearLayout>
            </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>
        </androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout>
    </androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView>
</layout>
